    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** standing as our clear analytical anchor. With a score of 80.21, he is the unequivocal start of the day; the combination of a favorable matchup against Pallante and the high-leverage stolen base environment created by the pitcher's slow delivery makes him our premier asset in a league where speed remains the scarcest commodity. He is joined in the \"Strong\" tier by **Caleb Durbin** (66.40) and **Lars Nootbaar** (62.26). Both benefit significantly from favorable platoon splits against right-handed pitching, with **Caleb Durbin** enjoying a park factor boost that elevates his floor well above the rest of the utility infield group.\n\nOur bench decisions today are largely dictated by the schedule, as stalwarts like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Trea Turner**, **Kyle Schwarber**, and **Christian Yelich** are inactive due to team off-days. This leaves our middle-tier depth players to carry the load. **Junior Caminero** (57.51) sits just outside the \"Strong\" threshold, hampered primarily by a tough matchup against Winn, whose 3.59 xERA provides a significant challenge. While **Junior Caminero** holds a solid xwOBA of 0.370, we are exercising caution given the difficulty of the opposing pitcher. Meanwhile, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Carson Benge**, and **Chase Meidroth** fall into the marginal category (47\u201352 range). **Jacob Gonzalez** gets a favorable look against Lowder\u2019s 4.86 xERA, but his position in the eight-hole acts as a drag on his potential for run production, keeping his score suppressed despite the exploitable matchup. We will monitor weather reports for all venues, but for now, the primary risk remains the high-skill arms of Weathers and Winn, which could stifle output for **Chase Meidroth** and **Junior Caminero** respectively.",
